                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12969: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-23T16:00:00+08:00">February 23, 1935—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 23—8:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>87. Reference Legation’s 83, February 20, 10 a.m., following telegram has been
                    received from the Consul General at Hankow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 23, noon. On February 22 China Inland Mission here submitted
                            correspondence indicating apparent increased gravity of the situation in
                            south Shensi, including telegram from Sian dated February 20th that
                            Hanchung (Mincheng) was reported taken by Red forces. British
                            missionaries attempting to evacuate Upper Han valley in Shensi. No
                            Americans in that area so far as known. On February 16th I telegraphed
                            Chairmen of Shensi and Kansu listing places where Americans reside and
                            requesting protection and assistance in evacuation if necessary.</p>
                        <p>Repeated to Nanking.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
